<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daijō-kan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">689–1885</span> · Also known as Great Council of State (Japan)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Daijō-kan, or Great Council of State, was the supreme organ of government in ritsuryō <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, set up under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiho-code">Taihō Code</a> of 701 and earlier codes. Headed by the chancellor and the ministers of the left and right, it supervised eight ministries and the provincial governors. Its power faded under the Fujiwara regents and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunates</a>, though it formally survived until 1885.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daijo-kan.jpg" alt="Daijō-kan" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philip Nilsson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imperial_Seal_of_Japan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
